## Phase 9 addendum — zone partitioning observed on real data / 2026-08-31

Recorded after the Phase 9 checkpoint had already consolidated and cleared this
file, so it sits here as raw material for the Phase 13 checkpoint rather than
being back-edited into INTERVIEW.md. It confirms, on real data, the weakness
INTERVIEW.md §15.4 already states from a constructed test.

### What was observed

Chasing a separate bug (the shift seed's 00:00-06:00 coverage gap, fixed in
`prisma/seed/technicians.ts` and pinned by `tests/shifts.test.ts`), a full
`/solve` run against `zippy_test` produced 1,317 feasible options and **zero
assignments in all three plans**. The same snapshot solved without
partitioning produced three.

Measured, one snapshot, 439 pending jobs, 102 units:

    technicians on shift at 03:20 local:  4  (tech-07, tech-14, tech-21, tech-28)
    of those, crewing a unit:             3  (unit-07@zone-kor, unit-08@zone-ind,
                                              unit-21@zone-whf)
    pending jobs by zone:  as-zone 335, ir-zone 52, rt-zone 52

    zonePartitionThreshold 1_000_000 → partitioned=false  feasible=1317  assigned=3
    zonePartitionThreshold       400 → partitioned=true   feasible=1317  assigned=0

Note the direction of the threshold, because it reads backwards at a glance:
partitioning fires when `optionCount > threshold`, so the LOW threshold is the
partitioned run and the high one is the whole-fleet matrix. 10,536 options were
generated, so 400 partitions and 1,000,000 does not.

### Why it happens, and why it is not a bug

`partitionByZone` matches a job only against units whose `zoneId` equals the
job's. Every pending job in that database sits in a test-fixture zone
(`as-zone`, `ir-zone`, `rt-zone`) and every crewed unit sits in a seeded zone
(`zone-kor`, `zone-ind`, `zone-whf`), so after partitioning no job shares a
matrix with any unit that could serve it. Unpartitioned, the three live units
are matched across zone boundaries and three jobs are served.

This is exactly the approximation `src/engine/solve.ts` documents and
`tests/solve.test.ts` asserts synthetically — a job near (here, across) a zone
boundary loses a better cross-zone option — showing up on real data rather than
in a constructed two-zone fixture. The behaviour is correct for the design.

### What it does and does not say about the design

It does NOT say partitioning is broken. It DOES say the approximation's cost is
not a rounding error at the tail: in a fleet whose crewed units and whose
pending jobs live in disjoint zones, partitioning takes assignments to zero
while a whole-fleet matrix still finds work. Two conditions produced that here,
both of which can occur in production:

- **Thin coverage.** At 03:20 only 4 of 31 technicians were on shift and only 3
  crewed a unit, so the fleet was small enough that losing any cross-zone
  option lost most of the answer. Partitioning is defended as a scale measure,
  but its damage is worst when the fleet is SMALL relative to the zone count —
  the opposite of the case it exists for.
- **Jobs and units in disjoint zones.** Here an artefact of fixture data, but
  the production analogue is real: a zone whose units are all committed while
  its neighbour has spare capacity.

### Open question for Phase 10

The evaluation harness measures how often this costs anything in a realistic
scenario, which is the number that decides whether the threshold is right, or
whether partitioning should be conditioned on fleet size rather than option
count alone. Until then this is a known approximation with one real-data
observation and no measured frequency — recorded here so the harness has a
concrete case to reproduce, not treated as a defect to fix ahead of the
measurement.

## Phase 10 — Evaluation harness / 2026-08-31 / Commit: "phase 10: evaluation harness"

### Built

`/evaluation`, importing the production engine throughout — no second solver:
- `scenarios/fleet.ts` — `buildSnapshot(shape, at)`, `FULL_FLEET`,
  `zoneSnapshots()`, `FleetShape`. Builds the evaluated fleet from the seed's
  own pure plan functions (`technicianPlan`, `shiftPlan`, `unitPlanEntries`,
  `planCrews`, `ZONES`), so the fleet under test IS the seeded fleet.
  `rebaseShift` moves seeded shift windows onto the scenario's day.
- `scenarios/index.ts` — the five scenarios (`normal-day`, `monsoon-surge`,
  `capability-scarcity`, `ambiguous-diagnosis`, `technician-dropout`),
  `buildScenario(name, seed, overrides)`, `SCENARIO_START`, Poisson arrivals,
  and the hidden `trueFault` drawn from each job's own distribution.
- `policies/index.ts` — `Policy`, `PolicyInput`, `PolicyDecision`, the four
  named policies and `policyByName`.
- `policies/greedy.ts` — `greedyPolicy(rank)`, the one loop behind fifo,
  nearest-unit and skill-aware.
- `policies/global.ts` — calls `solve()` and reads the BALANCED plan.
- `runner.ts` — `runScenario(scenario, policy, options?)`, the discrete-event
  loop; `RunResult`, `JobRecord`, `RunOptions`.
- `metrics.ts` — `computeMetrics`, `gini`; the eight named metrics plus
  `resolvedCount` and `strandedCount`.
- `report.ts` — `metricsTable`, `verdicts`, `timingSection`, `buildReport`.
- `cli.ts` — argument parsing, the sweep, stdout and `EVALUATION.md`.

Scripts: `eval`, `eval:sweep`, `eval:scenario`. Output: `EVALUATION.md`.

Engine changes, both small and both forced by the harness:
- `src/infra/routing.ts` — `TravelTimeSource` type and `haversineTravelTimes`,
  the existing `haversineFallback` exposed as a callable source.
- `src/engine/candidates.ts` — `generateCandidates` takes an optional
  `travelTimes`, defaulting to `travelTimeMatrix`.
- `src/engine/solve.ts` — `SolveInput.travelTimes` threaded through;
  `PlanAssignment` now carries the `option` it was chosen from.
- `src/commit/persistSolverRun.ts` — writes the option's real
  `estimatedTravelMinutes` / `coveredFaultProbability` / `missingCapabilities`
  instead of values reverse-engineered from the cost breakdown.
- `tsconfig.check.json` — a checking config covering src, tests, evaluation
  and prisma. `npm run typecheck` now uses it; `npm run build` still uses the
  narrow `tsconfig.json`. `npm run lint` now covers `evaluation` too.

Tests: `tests/evaluation.test.ts` (12). Suite total 251 across 18 files.
`tsc --noEmit`, `eslint src evaluation`, `npm run build` all clean. Two runs of
`--scenario monsoon-surge --seed 48291` are byte-identical on stdout.

### Decisions

- **Decision:** the harness passes `haversineTravelTimes` instead of calling
  OSRM.
  **Why:** invariant 10 says a decision's inputs must be local and
  deterministic "or the evaluation harness stops being reproducible". A
  scenario replayed 120 times cannot afford an HTTP round trip per job, and a
  run that fell back mid-way because the container hiccuped would produce two
  different answers from one seed — the exact failure the invariant names.
  **Alternative:** run against the real OSRM container.
  **Why not:** slow, and reproducibility becomes a property of infrastructure
  uptime rather than of the code.
  **Tradeoff:** absolute minutes are optimistic (straight line × road factor)
  and every option is flagged `degraded`. Stated in EVALUATION.md. It does not
  bias the comparison, because all four policies see identical numbers.

- **Decision:** all four policies run the production candidate generator and
  the production feasibility filter; only the CHOICE among feasible options
  varies.
  **Why:** a nearest-unit baseline that ignored capability constraints would
  lose for a reason unrelated to the objective — it would be losing because it
  sent a bike to a tow. Holding hard constraints identical is what makes the
  comparison about the thing being compared.
  **Alternative:** a simpler nearest baseline with no feasibility filter.
  **Why not:** it would be a strawman, and beating a strawman is not evidence.
  **Tradeoff:** the baselines are strong — skill-aware in particular optimises
  first-visit resolution directly — so global's margin is small or negative in
  several scenarios. That is the honest result.

- **Decision:** the global policy always reads the BALANCED plan.
  **Why:** `solve()` returns three plans for a dispatcher to choose between,
  and a harness has no dispatcher. Fixing the vector up front means the
  scoreboard is not chosen to suit the player.
  **Alternative:** report whichever of the three does best per scenario.
  **Why not:** that is picking the weight vector after seeing the results,
  which is the tuning the phase forbids.
  **Tradeoff:** FASTEST_RESOLUTION would very likely score better on the
  headline metric, so the reported figures understate what the solver can do.

- **Decision:** `solverWallClockP95` goes to stderr and into EVALUATION.md,
  not to stdout.
  **Why:** the phase requires byte-identical stdout for a fixed seed, and a
  millisecond measurement cannot be byte-identical. Decisions and simulated
  durations are reproducible; wall clock is not.
  **Alternative:** print it in the main table and accept that the determinism
  check fails, or quantise it and hope.
  **Why not:** the first gives up the property that makes the harness a
  measurement; the second is a rounding that still drifts.
  **Tradeoff:** the metric is not in the stdout table. It is measured, printed
  and written to the document — labelled as the one non-reproducible number.

- **Decision:** report `strandedCount` (jobs nobody was ever sent to)
  alongside every mean.
  **Why:** every mean is over jobs that FINISHED. A policy that strands a
  third of the board and resolves the easy two-thirds quickly posts a
  beautiful mean. The stranded count is the only thing between that number and
  a false claim.
  **Alternative:** report the means alone.
  **Why not:** it would make the worst-performing policy look best, which is
  precisely the flattering-by-filtering the phase forbids.
  **Tradeoff:** a wider table.

- **Decision:** `verdicts()` ranks on resolved count first, then on mean
  resolution — lexicographic, not a blend.
  **Why:** a faster average over a smaller set is not a better outcome, and
  the two quantities are not commensurable.
  **Alternative:** rank on mean resolution alone.
  **Why not:** it rewards stranding.
  **Tradeoff:** a policy that resolves one more job "wins" even if its mean is
  materially worse. The table prints both, so the reader can disagree.

### Concurrency

None. The harness is single-threaded, touches no database and no queue, and
every random draw happens when the scenario is built rather than while it
runs. That is a requirement rather than an accident: a concurrent harness
would not be reproducible, and reproducibility is the only thing that makes
its numbers admissible.

The simulated world does contain a race in shape — a unit finishing at the
same minute a job arrives — but the event loop orders those deterministically
(jobs are considered at each event minute, decisions applied in the order the
policy returned them), so it is not a race in the code.

### Weaknesses introduced

- **The diagnosis is perfectly calibrated.** `trueFault` is drawn FROM the
  distribution the solver sees, so a job believed 70% flat battery genuinely
  is one 70% of the time. Real intake is not calibrated, and a mis-calibrated
  belief would hurt skill-aware and global — the two policies that trust the
  distribution — more than nearest-unit. Every result here is therefore an
  upper bound on how well belief-driven policies do.
- **Travel time is straight-line, not routed.** Absolute minutes are
  optimistic; the comparison is unaffected.
- **Durations are the hand-set figures from `src/engine/durations.ts`**, still
  unfitted to any real job data. Every minute reported inherits that.
- **The sweep varies three knobs at six points, not a grid.** It shows the
  result does not hold everywhere, which is the honest finding, but it does
  not isolate WHICH knob is responsible at any given point.
- **`solverWallClockP95` is a p95 over as few as a dozen decisions** in the
  smaller scenarios. It is a real measurement of a small sample, not a
  latency figure to quote.
- **Unit utilisation is low (3-16%) in every run**, because a 24-unit fleet is
  oversized for 60 jobs and because stranded jobs consume no capacity. It is
  not a meaningful capacity signal at these volumes.
- **The harness measures BALANCED only.** Nothing has compared the three
  weight vectors against each other, so "BALANCED" remains a name rather than
  a measured balance point — the Phase 8 weakness is unchanged.

### Concepts now genuinely in use

Discrete-event simulation with an event queue rather than a time step, and why
jumping to the next event is both faster and no less true; replaying one
seeded world against several policies so the only variable is the policy;
hidden ground truth (`trueFault`) versus believed distribution as the
mechanism that makes first-visit failure and re-dispatch emerge rather than be
asserted; the Gini coefficient as a distributional measure, and the specific
way excluding zero-earners falsifies it; percentile reporting over a small
sample and the honesty that requires; separating reproducible output from
inherently non-reproducible measurement; dependency injection of an
infrastructure boundary (travel time) as the thing that makes a pure engine
replayable; differential measurement — running one scenario twice with a
single knob changed — as the way to price an approximation.

### Interview angle

- *Your evaluation says the global policy loses two of five scenarios. Why
  ship it?* Because the harness also says WHY, and the reason is not the
  objective function. Running monsoon-surge twice with nothing changed but the
  zone-partition threshold: partitioned resolves 58/180, the whole-fleet
  matrix resolves 69/180. Partitioning — an O(n³) mitigation added in Phase 9
  with an explicitly unmeasured price — is what costs global its throughput.
  The objective is fine; the scale approximation on top of it is what needs
  work, and now there is a number attached to that.
- *How do you know the harness measures your real dispatcher and not a
  simplified copy of it?* `evaluation/policies/global.ts` is thirty lines and
  calls `solve()`. The greedy baselines call `generateCandidates` and
  `checkFeasibility` — the same functions the HTTP route uses. A test asserts
  those imports exist, and there is no cost function, no feasibility filter and
  no assignment algorithm anywhere under `/evaluation`.

### What broke

- **`npm run typecheck` had never checked the harness, or the tests.**
  `tsconfig.json` carries `rootDir: "src"` and `include: ["src/**/*"]` because
  it is the BUILD config, and `typecheck` was `tsc --noEmit` against it — so
  everything outside `src/` was invisible to the compiler. The harness was
  written against fault codes that do not exist (`WHEEL_DAMAGE`,
  `ELECTRICAL_FAULT`, `BATTERY_FAILURE`, `KEY_LOCKED_IN`, `ACCIDENT_DAMAGE`,
  `FUEL_CONTAMINATION`, `KEY_LOST`) and compiled clean. At runtime
  `distributionOf` normalised every unknown key to zero, so those jobs carried
  a distribution that was all zeros or a single fault, and a complete table of
  plausible-looking numbers was printed from them — 100% first-visit
  resolution and 0% second dispatch across every policy and scenario, which is
  what made it obvious something was wrong. `Partial<Record<FaultCode, number>>`
  rejects all seven the moment the file is actually checked. Fixed by adding
  `tsconfig.check.json` (src + tests + evaluation + prisma, noEmit) and
  pointing `typecheck` at it, then correcting the codes. The same config
  immediately surfaced two pre-existing `noUncheckedIndexedAccess` errors in
  `prisma/seed/technicians.ts` that had never been compiled either.
  The general lesson: a build config and a check config want different
  `include`s, and sharing one silently narrows the check to what ships.
- **The first table was measured on jobs with no fault at all.** Worth stating
  separately from the type error that caused it, because the failure mode is
  the dangerous one: nothing crashed, nothing warned, and the output looked
  entirely reasonable. 100% first-visit resolution should have been read as
  "the mechanism that produces second dispatches never fires" rather than as a
  good result, and it was not, until the numbers were compared against what
  the scenarios were supposed to contain.
- **`SLA_UNREACHABLE` permanently strands a late job, and the harness found
  it.** Once a job passes its promised window every option for it is rejected,
  so it can never be dispatched — the queue drops it rather than serving it
  late. Measured directly: a queued ambiguous-diagnosis job has 2 feasible
  options at +0 min and 0 at +30 min, with `SLA_UNREACHABLE` the only new
  code. That is where almost every `stranded` job in the table comes from
  (105-118 of 180 at monsoon-surge). NOT fixed in this phase: it is a Phase 7
  design decision, changing a hard filter is out of scope for a phase whose
  job is to measure, and it hits all four policies identically so the
  comparison stands. Recorded as the harness's first real finding about
  production code.
- **First-visit resolution is 93-100% for every policy**, which means the
  escalation term in the cost function is nearly inert. The capability filter
  already requires a crew to cover the contending faults, so a crew that
  passes feasibility can almost always finish the job. The second-dispatch
  risk the objective prices is largely a risk the filter upstream has already
  removed — an overlap between Phase 7 and Phase 8 that neither phase could
  see on its own.

### Open invariant concern — a job whose SLA window closes while queued

NOT FIXED IN THIS PHASE, deliberately. Phase 10's job is to measure, and
changing a hard filter is a Phase 7 design change. Recorded here in full so
Phase 13 inherits it as a decision to make rather than a bug to rediscover.

**What is correct.** `SLA_UNREACHABLE` is a hard filter returning a reason
code, not a numeric penalty. That is exactly what invariant 3 requires, and
the reasoning behind it holds: an option that cannot meet the contracted
response window is not a worse option, it is one that breaks a promise, and a
promise is not something a cost term should be able to outvote. Nothing about
the filter's SHAPE is wrong.

**What is wrong.** The filter is evaluated against `now`, and `now` moves. A
job that is still unassigned when its window closes does not merely lose the
options it had — it loses ALL options, permanently, because every future
evaluation compares an arrival time against a deadline that is already in the
past. The job stops appearing in any plan, for any unit, forever.

Measured (`evaluation/`, ambiguous-diagnosis, seed 20260901): one queued job
has 2 feasible options at +0 min and 0 at +30 min, with `SLA_UNREACHABLE` the
only newly-appearing rejection code. Across monsoon-surge that mechanism
accounts for most of the 105-118 stranded jobs per policy.

**Why that is an invariant 8 violation, not just an unlucky outcome.**
Invariant 8: every degraded state is visible to an operator. A job dropping
out of candidate generation is about as degraded as a dispatch system gets —
a stranded motorist nobody is coming for — and it currently surfaces NOWHERE.
It is not a `JobEvent`, not an alert, not a row on any dispatcher surface. It
is visible only as an absence: the job stops being mentioned in solver output.
The one place it is recorded at all is a `dispatch_options` row with
`rejection_code = 'SLA_UNREACHABLE'`, which reads identically to the ordinary
and entirely healthy case of one unit being too far away. Nothing
distinguishes "this unit cannot make it" from "no unit can ever make it
again".

The operational consequence is the wrong one in every direction: the system
abandons the customer it has already failed, and it does so quietly, which is
precisely the failure mode the invariant exists to forbid. Missing a
45-minute promise is a billing and escalation problem; never arriving is a
different kind of problem, and the code currently converts the first into the
second without telling anyone.

**Phase 13 does not currently catch this.** The re-solve trigger list
specified for `src/domain/replanTriggers.ts` is:

    diagnosis changed · technician declined · technician offline ·
    unit breakdown · destination declined · driver moved materially ·
    safety level changed · job cancelled

All eight are events where something about the WORLD changed. The SLA case is
not in the list and would not be caught by anything in it, because nothing
about the world changed — only the clock advanced. It is the one trigger whose
cause is the passage of time rather than an event, which is likely why it was
missed.

**Proposed for Phase 13 — an explicit ninth trigger.** Add
`SLA_WINDOW_CLOSED_WHILE_QUEUED` to `replanTriggers.ts`, fired when a job that
is still unassigned crosses its `slaPolicy.responseMinutes` deadline. The
trigger must NOT quietly re-solve and hope, because a re-solve at that moment
returns exactly what it returned a minute earlier: nothing. Instead it should
raise an **APPROVE-tier `ActionRequest`** — the tier that blocks pending
sign-off, with an approver recorded — carrying the reason, the elapsed
overrun, and the fact that every option is now filtered by a deadline rather
than by capacity. The dispatcher then makes the call the system cannot: serve
it late, re-contract the SLA, escalate to a partner, or cancel with the
customer told.

APPROVE rather than RECOMMEND because the decision is a commercial one about
a promise that has already been broken, and nothing in the engine has the
standing to make it. AUTO would be plainly wrong: silently relaxing a
contracted deadline is the single clearest example of "an AUTO action doing
something a human would want to review".

**One tension to resolve when it is built, not now.** APPROVE blocks until a
human signs off, so a job that is already late then waits longer while it sits
in an approval queue. Whether the right shape is a blocking APPROVE, or an
AUTO relaxation that dispatches immediately and raises a RECOMMEND for the
commercial follow-up, is a genuine design question. The measurement above says
only that the current behaviour — silence, and permanent infeasibility — is
the one option that is definitely wrong.

## Phase 11 — Holds and real concurrency / 2026-08-31 / Commit: "phase 11: holds and concurrency"

### Built

`src/commit/holds.ts` — `createHold`, `releaseHold`, `consumeHold`,
`DEFAULT_HOLD_TTL_MS` (2 min). Types `HoldOutcome`, `ReleaseOutcome`,
`ConsumeOutcome`. Three hand-written conditional UPDATEs, values bound by
Prisma's tagged template — no interpolation, no `$executeRawUnsafe` anywhere
in the repo.

`src/commit/offers.ts` — `proposeOffer`, `respondToOffer`, `timeoutOffer`,
`DEFAULT_OFFER_TTL_MS` (45s). One private `transitionOffer` behind all three,
because accept, decline and timeout differ only in the status they move to.

`src/workers/offerExpiry.ts` — now real work: `processOfferExpiry` calls
`timeoutOffer`. `proposeOffer` schedules its own expiry after the transaction
commits, and takes an injectable `scheduleExpiry` so a Postgres-only test does
not need a broker.

Test infrastructure:
- `tests/concurrency/harness.ts` — `startPostgres()` (testcontainers, real
  `prisma migrate deploy`), `seedFixture()`.
- `tests/concurrency/holds.test.ts` — the five named races plus the stale-read
  test (7 tests).
- `tests/concurrency/mutation.test.ts` — the version check deleted on purpose
  (2 tests), skipped when `CI` is set.
- `vitest.concurrency.config.ts` — its own config: long timeouts,
  `fileParallelism: false`. `vitest.config.ts` now EXCLUDES
  `tests/concurrency/**` so the default suite stays fast.
- `test:concurrency` is now `vitest run --config vitest.concurrency.config.ts`,
  and `test:all` chains it after `test` so "all tests pass" is one command.

Suite totals: 251 across 18 files (default), 9 across 2 files (concurrency,
17s wall clock with a warm image). `tsc --noEmit`, `eslint src evaluation`,
`npm run build` all clean.

### Decisions

- **Decision:** the conditional UPDATE is hand-written SQL, not `updateMany`.
  **Why:** the WHERE clause IS the concurrency argument. Someone asking "where
  is the race handled?" should read one statement, not reconstruct it from an
  ORM call and its generated SQL.
  **Alternative:** `prisma.unit.updateMany({ where: { id, status, version } })`,
  which compiles to the same thing and also reports a count.
  **Why not:** it is equally correct and less legible at the one point in the
  codebase where legibility is the whole product.
  **Tradeoff:** raw SQL is not type-checked against the schema, so a renamed
  column becomes a runtime error rather than a compile error. Accepted for
  three statements; it would not be accepted for thirty.

- **Decision:** `releaseHold` frees the unit only inside the branch that
  actually moved the hold out of ACTIVE, and only `WHERE status = 'HELD'`.
  **Why:** invariant 6 — every compensation is idempotent. Three different
  things release a hold (dispatcher, cleanup sweep, Phase 12 compensation) and
  they can overlap. A second release must not free a unit that has since been
  legitimately dispatched to someone else.
  **Alternative:** set the unit AVAILABLE unconditionally on every release
  call.
  **Why not:** that is a double-free. By the time the second call arrives the
  unit may be ON_JOB, and a blind write would hand out a van that is already
  driving somewhere.
  **Tradeoff:** "released" and "the unit is free" are two facts that a caller
  cannot infer from one another; the second release returns
  `ALREADY_RESOLVED` and says nothing about the unit.

- **Decision:** `consumeHold` folds the expiry check into the same WHERE
  clause as the status check.
  **Why:** checking `expiresAt > now` separately and then updating reopens
  exactly the window the conditional update exists to close — the hold can
  expire between the two statements.
  **Alternative:** read the hold, check expiry in TypeScript, then update.
  **Why not:** a check-then-act with a real gap in it.
  **Tradeoff:** the failure reason has to be reconstructed on the losing path
  (an ACTIVE row that failed the update failed on expiry), which is a small
  amount of re-reading to produce a good error.

- **Decision:** `proposeOffer` schedules the expiry job AFTER its transaction
  commits, and the scheduler is an injectable parameter.
  **Why:** scheduling inside the transaction would queue an expiry for an
  offer a rollback then erased — the same publish-after-commit rule the
  realtime outbox exists for, applied to a queue. Injectable because the
  concurrency suite is proving a Postgres race and should not need a Redis to
  do it.
  **Alternative:** schedule inside the transaction; mock BullMQ in the test.
  **Why not:** the first writes a phantom job; the second puts a mock in the
  one suite whose entire premise is that nothing is mocked.
  **Tradeoff:** a crash between commit and schedule leaves an offer with no
  expiry — it would sit PROPOSED forever. Not handled this phase; the cleanup
  sweep is the obvious home for it and does not do it yet. Recorded below.

- **Decision:** the concurrency suite gets its own vitest config and is
  excluded from `npm test`.
  **Why:** it wants the opposite of what the default suite wants — a real
  container, minutes of timeout, one file at a time. Two suites with genuinely
  different needs get two configs rather than one config full of conditionals.
  **Alternative:** one config, with the concurrency tests skipped unless an
  env var is set.
  **Why not:** a suite that is skipped by default is a suite nobody notices
  has broken.
  **Tradeoff:** `npm test` no longer runs everything, so "all tests pass" now
  requires two commands. Stated in the scripts rather than assumed.

### Concurrency

This is the phase, so the section is the point rather than a footnote. All
five proved against a real Postgres started by the test, with `Promise.all`,
no mocks.

- **Scenario:** two dispatchers hold the same unit.
  **What races:** two `UPDATE units ... WHERE id AND status='AVAILABLE' AND
  version=$2` against one row.
  **Why dangerous:** both told "you got it" means two vans to one breakdown
  and one breakdown with none.
  **How handled:** the check and the claim are one statement. Postgres blocks
  the second UPDATE on the first's row lock and re-evaluates its WHERE against
  the committed row; it matches nothing and reports zero rows.
  **Assumption relied on:** READ COMMITTED, which is Postgres's default and
  what this project runs at. At that level a blocked UPDATE re-reads the row
  rather than failing — the guarantee would be different (serialisation
  failure, needing retry) under REPEATABLE READ.

- **Scenario:** a technician accepts as the offer expires.
  **What races:** `respondToOffer` and `timeoutOffer` on one
  `technician_offers` row.
  **Why dangerous:** the two outcomes are contradictory, not merely different
  — a job both assigned and released for reassignment sends two vans.
  **How handled:** both are the same conditional UPDATE guarded by
  `status = 'PROPOSED'`. Whoever moves the row first wins; the loser is told
  what actually happened instead of erroring.
  **Assumption relied on:** nothing else writes that column. Tested 12 times
  in a row, because a race that resolves correctly once may only have resolved
  correctly because one side was slower.

- **Scenario:** release called twice.
  **What races:** two `releaseHold` calls on one hold.
  **Why dangerous:** a double-free returns a unit that may already be on
  another job.
  **How handled:** conditional on `(id, status='ACTIVE', version)`; only the
  winner frees the unit, and only `WHERE status='HELD'`.
  **Assumption relied on:** none beyond the above. Asserted by the unit's
  version moving exactly twice — a double-free is invisible in the status but
  shows up as a third increment.

- **Scenario:** the hold expires mid-commit.
  **What races:** the cleanup sweep expiring a hold while the saga tries to
  consume it.
  **Why dangerous:** consuming an expired claim dispatches a unit the system
  already gave up.
  **How handled:** `consumeHold` requires `status='ACTIVE' AND expires_at >
  now` in one statement, and the unit stays HELD rather than moving to
  ON_JOB.
  **Assumption relied on:** the sweep only ever moves ACTIVE → EXPIRED.

- **Scenario:** 50 concurrent holds on 10 units.
  **What races:** five contenders per unit, all reading the same version.
  **Why dangerous:** the aggregate version of the first case.
  **How handled:** as above. Exactly 10 succeed, 40 lose, one ACTIVE hold per
  unit.
  **Assumption relied on:** none.

### Weaknesses introduced

- **A crash between `proposeOffer`'s commit and its `scheduleOfferExpiry` call
  leaves an offer that never expires.** It would sit PROPOSED forever, holding
  a technician's attention and blocking the job. The cleanup sweep is the
  natural place to catch it (`status='PROPOSED' AND expires_at < now`) and does
  not do it yet.
- **Nothing calls any of this.** `POST /solve` still returns plans and reserves
  nothing; no route creates a hold or an offer. The whole module is exercised
  only by its tests until Phase 12's saga wires it into a real dispatch.
- **A hold is not linked to its offer.** `UnitHold.dispatchOfferId` and
  `TechnicianOffer.dispatchOfferId` are both still null — `proposeOffer` takes
  a `holdId` and ignores it. Declining an offer therefore does not release the
  hold it belonged to; something has to, and today nothing does except the
  expiry sweep.
- **`consumeHold` throws a hand-constructed `PrismaClientKnownRequestError`**
  when the hold is consumable but its unit is not HELD. That state should be
  impossible, so the throw is a tripwire rather than a handled path, but
  borrowing Prisma's error type for a condition Prisma did not raise is a
  small lie to anything catching by type.
- **Raw SQL is not checked against the schema.** A column rename in
  `schema.prisma` compiles fine and fails at runtime in the three statements
  that matter most.
- **The offer TTL and hold TTL are unmeasured** — 45 seconds and 2 minutes,
  named as guesses in their own comments. Nothing has observed a real
  technician's response latency, though `response_latency_ms` is now recorded
  on every answered offer so Phase 13 can.
- **`npm test` no longer means "all tests"** — it excludes the concurrency
  suite so it can stay fast. `test:all` (`npm run test && npm run
  test:concurrency`) exists so the full run is still one command, but the
  weakness is not fully closed: the short name is still the partial one, so a
  contributor who types the obvious thing gets the partial answer, and CI has
  to be pointed at `test:all` deliberately. Renaming the scripts so the safe
  option is the default would close it properly and is not done.

### Concepts now genuinely in use

Optimistic concurrency as a conditional UPDATE rather than a lock, and the
specific reason a row lock is wrong here (the transaction after a dispatch
decision waits on a human); READ COMMITTED's re-evaluation of a blocked
UPDATE's WHERE clause, which is the mechanism that makes a status guard
sufficient for simultaneity and insufficient for staleness; the ABA problem,
and the version column as the thing that distinguishes "the row I decided on"
from "a row that has been round the block since"; idempotent compensation as
a shape (conditional update, report what you found, never throw);
testcontainers as the difference between a property that is checked and a
property that is provable on a machine that has never seen the project;
mutation testing applied to a concurrency guard, including the case where the
mutation does NOT break the test and the finding is that the test was aimed at
the wrong clause.

### Interview angle

- *Two dispatchers click the same van. What stops the double-booking, exactly?*
  One statement: `UPDATE units SET status='HELD', version=version+1 WHERE
  id=$1 AND status='AVAILABLE' AND version=$2`. There is no window between
  checking and claiming because Postgres evaluates the WHERE and applies the
  UPDATE atomically, and the second writer blocks on the first's row lock and
  then re-reads. Zero rows affected is how the loser finds out. Proved with a
  real `Promise.all` against a container the test started — and at 50-on-10,
  exactly 10 succeed.
- *You have a version column AND a status check. Isn't one of them redundant?*
  No, and I only know that because I deleted one and measured. Removing
  `AND version = $2` does NOT break the two-dispatchers test: the status check
  alone handles simultaneity. What the version column catches is the stale
  read — a dispatcher whose plan was computed before the unit was held and
  released by someone else. It is AVAILABLE again, so a status check waves the
  claim through; the version says the world moved. The test that pins that is
  a different test from the one the build plan predicted would fail.

### What broke

- **A Phase 5 job-id bug that had never been executed.** `offerExpiryJobId`
  returned `offer-expiry:${offerId}`, and BullMQ rejects a custom job id
  containing `:` — it uses colons as separators in its own Redis key layout.
  The function was written in Phase 5 as plumbing with no caller, so the
  validation error sat undiscovered until this phase made real offers schedule
  real expiries and the concurrency suite hit it on the first run. Fixed to a
  hyphen. `deduplication.id` has no such restriction, which is why the
  solver's `zone:...` key has been fine all along — worth knowing, because the
  two look interchangeable and are not.
- **THE MUTATION DID NOT BREAK WHAT THE BUILD PLAN SAID IT WOULD, and that is
  the most useful thing in this phase.** The plan states: delete
  `AND version = $2`, and the two-dispatchers test MUST fail. It does not.
  Measured directly — two concurrent unsafe holds on one unit still come back
  `HELD` / `LOST_RACE` with exactly one ACTIVE hold row.

  The reason is that `AND status = 'AVAILABLE'` is itself a conditional guard.
  At READ COMMITTED, two UPDATEs on one row do not both proceed: the second
  blocks on the first's row lock, and when the first commits the second
  RE-EVALUATES its WHERE clause against the newly committed row. It sees
  `status = 'HELD'`, matches nothing, reports zero rows. Simultaneity is
  handled by the status clause alone, and in that scenario the version clause
  does no work whatsoever.

  So the original test 1 was passing for a reason that had nothing to do with
  the mechanism it was believed to be testing — the same failure recorded in
  Phase 8's comparator and Phase 7's ordering, for the third time. What the
  version column actually protects is the ABA case: read AVAILABLE at version
  4, somebody else holds and releases it, it is AVAILABLE again at version 6,
  and a status-only check cannot tell "still the unit I chose" from "a unit
  that has been round the block since". Measured: with the version check the
  stale claim is refused; without it, it succeeds.

  Two things were added rather than one. `holds.test.ts` gained a direct
  stale-version test so the main suite is load-bearing for the version column,
  and `mutation.test.ts` asserts BOTH results — that deleting the clause does
  not break simultaneity, and that it does break staleness — so the negative
  result is pinned too. If a future Postgres or isolation-level change made
  the unsafe version start double-booking, that test fails and the explanation
  above needs rewriting, which is the right way to hold a claim about database
  semantics.
- **`npx` needed a shell on Windows, and passing arguments through a shell is
  deprecated (DEP0190).** The migration step ran `execFileSync('npx', [...],
  { shell: true })` and printed a deprecation warning on every run; switching
  to `npx.cmd` without a shell simply failed to launch. Resolved by running
  Prisma's own CLI entrypoint on `process.execPath` —
  `createRequire(import.meta.url).resolve('prisma/build/index.js')` — which
  needs no shell on any platform and fails loudly if a Prisma upgrade moves
  the file.
- **The first container wait strategy would have been flaky.** Postgres's
  entrypoint starts the server, stops it to run init scripts, and starts it
  again, so waiting for one "ready to accept connections" line returns a
  server that is about to disappear. The wait is for the second occurrence.
  Caught while writing it rather than by a flake, but it is the standard way
  this goes wrong.
